    "updated_at": "2019-05-03T16:10:31Z",
    "body": "> I'm not sure how to test whether this would match current implementations, though (since I'm not sure how to trigger the suspended state programmatically).\r\n\r\nIf by \"suspended state\", you mean \"the event-loop won't be running any tasks for that document\", there is a [recently-merged wpt test](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/15224/files) that puts a \"fully active\" document into \"active\" mode, and then back to \"fully active\", and makes assertion about tasks not running while the document is in \"active\" mode, and then running once it gets back into \"fully active\" mode. \r\n\r\nI guess that test could be used as a convenient template to write a test regarding the \"passage of time\"(or the lack thereof) as it relates to timers(it is using storage notifications instead, as the relevant \"task\"). \r\n\r\n ",
