    "created_at": "2019-05-03T16:00:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-03T16:00:25Z",
    "body": "> I'm not sure. @bzbarsky?\r\n\r\nThere are basically two ways to handle timers across bfcache suspending a document:\r\n\r\n1) Treat the whole thing like one would treat severe jank on the main thread causing timers to not fire on time.  This corresponds to time passing while suspended, which means that when you unsuspend there can be a blizzard of timeouts and intervals firing, with no time between them even though they were initially set for a variety of different times.\r\n\r\n2) Do what the spec says now (and Gecko implements): when suspending, record for each timer how much longer it needs to go before firing, then restore that state when un-suspending.  This isn't quite the spec formulation, but is what Gecko implements, and the observable behavior is, I believe, equivalent.\r\n\r\nIn terms of the original design criteria around the bfcache, option 2 is vastly superior: the whole point is to have a fast user experience when the user clicks the \"back\" button, and having to suddenly run a whole bunch of timer-triggered script at that exact point because all the timers have expired would be quite detrimental to the user experience.\r\n\r\nBy the way, I vaguely recall that handling of OS sleep (i.e. \"laptop is closed for a while\") is also along the lines of option 2, though I would need to do some experiments to verify.  Certainly we've had bug reports a long time ago, before timers were switched from wall-clock time to monotonic time, about a timer-firing blizzard when the OS woke up, and I _think_ that got addressed.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
